1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:02,907
February 1991, tank crews of the U.S.

2
00:00:02,908 --> 00:00:07,700
Armored Divisions lie in wait in the desert of southern Iraq,

3
00:00:08,120 --> 00:00:11,440
ready to face the heavy armor of Saddam Hussein's Republican Guard.

4
00:00:11,441 --> 00:00:17,240
But the tank that would spearhead the assault was untested on the battlefield and shrouded in controversy.

5
00:00:18,060 --> 00:00:23,440
March 2003, the brutes of the battlefield roll across the Iraqi desert once again.

6
00:00:24,040 --> 00:00:29,060
Destination Baghdad, now M1 Abrams Supertank on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:42,000
The Battle Tank

8
00:00:42,001 --> 00:00:44,800
The development of the battle tank revolutionized warfare.

9
00:00:45,720 --> 00:00:49,780
They were designed to break the stalemate in the trenches of the First World War.

10
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:54,520
But during World War II, the tank came of age.

11
00:00:54,521 --> 00:01:02,240
Fast-moving panzers led the Nazis' stunning Blitzkrieg victories of 1940.

12
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:09,940
In 1941, more than 3,500 German panzers led the Nazi invasion of Soviet Russia.

13
00:01:14,280 --> 00:01:22,320
The Russian T-34, the standard battle tank of the Soviet army, was produced in tens of thousands in Tankograd,

14
00:01:22,321 --> 00:01:26,800
a huge new specialized production center hidden deep in Siberia.

15
00:01:29,320 --> 00:01:33,880
These superb machines, a match for almost anything the Germans had,

16
00:01:34,240 --> 00:01:36,740
led the Russian advance from Kursk to Berlin.

17
00:01:42,700 --> 00:01:45,600
When the United States mobilized for war,

18
00:01:46,020 --> 00:01:48,680
Chrysler built a giant tank arsenal in Detroit.

19
00:01:49,480 --> 00:01:54,740
Soon, tens of thousands of M4 Sherman tanks began pouring off the production line.

20
00:01:55,640 --> 00:01:58,940
The Sherman became the universal Allied tank of the war,

21
00:01:59,480 --> 00:02:03,560
leading the breakout from Normandy and the final onslaught against Nazi Germany.

22
00:02:03,561 --> 00:02:15,560
The first sight of a Sherman

23
00:02:15,561 --> 00:02:17,140
There it was

24
00:02:17,141 --> 00:02:18,780
A huge tank

25
00:02:18,781 --> 00:02:20,920
A veritable Rolls Royce

26
00:02:20,921 --> 00:02:23,240
Compared with what we'd been used to

27
00:02:23,241 --> 00:02:27,000
We knew we had the arsenal of democracy

28
00:02:27,001 --> 00:02:28,860
The pride was that

29
00:02:28,861 --> 00:02:30,320
Americans could do it

30
00:02:30,321 --> 00:02:31,980
We could manufacture these tanks

31
00:02:31,981 --> 00:02:36,220
And we were told they were good tanks and they would help us win the war.

32
00:02:36,900 --> 00:02:45,140
In late 1944, the German High Command concentrated their war industry on the development of superior weapons.

33
00:02:46,380 --> 00:02:54,620
In an intensive effort to defeat the Allied forces, they produced heavier and heavier tanks like the Tiger and the King Tiger.

34
00:02:56,280 --> 00:03:01,840
These tanks were feared by the Allies, but there were too few of them to turn the tide of war.

35
00:03:01,980 --> 00:03:10,300
The design of tanks had always been a compromise between armor, firepower, and mobility.

36
00:03:10,620 --> 00:03:14,880
The heavier the armor, the better the protection, but slower the speed.

37
00:03:15,440 --> 00:03:18,600
The bigger the gun, the heavier the casing needed to carry it.

38
00:03:19,180 --> 00:03:23,260
Victory or defeat depended on a balance between these characteristics.

39
00:03:23,261 --> 00:03:32,260
During the Cold War, the Soviet Union was years ahead of the West in the development of tank technology.

40
00:03:32,261 --> 00:03:39,260
They had produced a range of low-profile designs that were harder to identify and to hit.

41
00:03:42,260 --> 00:03:51,260
The US and West Germany, now allies, collaborated to produce a new tank of the century that would incorporate the latest scientific developments.

42
00:03:53,260 --> 00:03:59,680
By 1969, with over $400 million spent, the project was way over budget.

43
00:04:00,220 --> 00:04:01,460
Congress pulled the plug.

44
00:04:04,700 --> 00:04:08,540
But the U.S. still needed a new generation of battle tanks.

45
00:04:09,660 --> 00:04:15,520
In 1972, both Chrysler and General Motors were asked to come up with new designs.

46
00:04:17,840 --> 00:04:21,460
The United States hadn't had a new tank for quite a number of years,

47
00:04:21,461 --> 00:04:27,820
and the Army felt the need to incorporate the knowledge that was available then

48
00:04:27,821 --> 00:04:34,440
into a new tank that would give them better firepower, more mobility and reliability.

49
00:04:35,320 --> 00:04:41,120
The Army did, I think, a very intelligent, very smart job of managing the program.

50
00:04:41,440 --> 00:04:46,980
The first thing they did was to get a group of seasoned, experienced tankers together at Fort Knox

51
00:04:46,981 --> 00:04:49,180
and asked these gentlemen,

52
00:04:49,540 --> 00:04:51,820
what is it you'd like to have in the next generation tank?

53
00:04:52,540 --> 00:04:55,780
It's somewhat similar to putting a kid in a candy store.

54
00:04:56,440 --> 00:05:01,900
And what came out of that was a very tough and demanding set of requirements.

55
00:05:03,720 --> 00:05:07,780
World events would also play an important role in shaping the design.

56
00:05:08,100 --> 00:05:15,300
In October 1973, the Middle East War saw the largest tank versus tank battle since World War II.

57
00:05:16,980 --> 00:05:22,680
Israeli armored brigades fought with British Centurion tanks and the latest American patents.

58
00:05:24,060 --> 00:05:29,220
The Egyptian and the Syrian forces were armed with a modern Soviet T-62 tank.

59
00:05:31,020 --> 00:05:35,920
The Middle East War proved the tank remained the dominant weapon on the modern battlefield.

60
00:05:38,540 --> 00:05:43,680
The U.S. Army was now more committed than ever to design a new main battle tank.

61
00:05:43,681 --> 00:05:49,680
For U.S. tank designers, the war provided a unique opportunity.

62
00:05:50,420 --> 00:05:56,240
They closely tracked the strengths and weaknesses of the armored divisions and brought the results to the design table.

63
00:05:59,960 --> 00:06:08,200
Another factor that would affect the tank's overall design was the new top-secret British Choban armor, codenamed Burlington by the Army.

64
00:06:08,201 --> 00:06:17,640
In 1978, successful impact tests on the secret Burlington armor were held at the Army's Aberdeen Proofing Ground.

65
00:06:18,900 --> 00:06:21,360
There are two basic kinds of tank ammunition.

66
00:06:21,920 --> 00:06:23,440
One is kinetic energy.

67
00:06:23,920 --> 00:06:28,120
Brute force pushes a hole through the armor, and it's very effective.

68
00:06:28,121 --> 00:06:38,800
The other type of anti-tank round is called a chemical energy round.

69
00:06:39,360 --> 00:06:44,200
Molten metal comes out of the front of the shell in a finger-sized jet

70
00:06:44,201 --> 00:06:48,220
that will burn a hole through several feet of steel.

71
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:50,500
How can we defeat both?

72
00:06:50,700 --> 00:06:52,540
Well, the chopper armor did that.

73
00:06:58,120 --> 00:07:04,160
November 12, 1976, Chrysler was declared the winner of the new contract.

74
00:07:05,540 --> 00:07:13,220
They beat General Motors by designing a turret that reduced costs and by incorporating a revolutionary gas turbine engine.

75
00:07:13,940 --> 00:07:16,800
It was the beginning of a new era in tank design.

76
00:07:18,280 --> 00:07:23,520
I recall very well the announcement that we had won.

77
00:07:23,521 --> 00:07:26,700
and I stepped on top of a drafting table,

78
00:07:27,140 --> 00:07:32,520
and I can still feel the resounding applause

79
00:07:32,521 --> 00:07:35,880
knowing that the Army had chosen our group to proceed with it,

80
00:07:35,881 --> 00:07:41,100
and it was just a moment of elation that was hard to capture in words.

81
00:07:42,120 --> 00:07:44,340
But the real winner was the U.S. Army.

82
00:07:45,020 --> 00:07:49,220
In just two years, they made a sensational leap forward in tank technology.

83
00:07:49,221 --> 00:07:57,000
In 1978, the first of this new generation of tanks rolled off the production line.

84
00:07:59,320 --> 00:08:02,740
The M1 Abrams Supertank was born.

85
00:08:10,420 --> 00:08:12,480
It was quite an impressive vehicle.

86
00:08:13,100 --> 00:08:17,560
You compared it against the M60, which was our top of the line at the time.

87
00:08:17,561 --> 00:08:21,900
It's lower, it's more angular, its design looks more menacing,

88
00:08:22,580 --> 00:08:24,940
its armor is not just all steel,

89
00:08:25,440 --> 00:08:28,700
it's designed to defeat a wider range of munitions,

90
00:08:29,240 --> 00:08:33,820
and its fire control system, similar to the M60, was in fact superior.

91
00:08:33,821 --> 00:08:37,800
So, especially when you couple it with a jet engine that's 1,500 horsepower,

92
00:08:38,580 --> 00:08:43,200
this beast moved faster, more quickly, was more survivable,

93
00:08:43,640 --> 00:08:46,060
and was at least as lethal as its predecessors.

94
00:08:47,560 --> 00:08:54,200
With the birth of this terrifying fighting machine, the battlefield would never be the same again.

95
00:08:57,040 --> 00:08:58,720
After World War II, the U.S.

96
00:08:58,721 --> 00:09:02,320
Sherman tank continued to serve armies throughout the world.

97
00:09:02,321 --> 00:09:09,440
Taken to the battlefield as recently as 1973, M1 Abrams Supertank will return on Modern Marvels.

98
00:09:09,441 --> 00:09:17,520
In 1980, the new M1 Abrams Supertank entered military service.

99
00:09:18,140 --> 00:09:20,500
But its early days were troubled and controversial.

100
00:09:21,420 --> 00:09:26,800
It was the world's top high-tech war machine, but many doubted its combat survivability.

101
00:09:29,200 --> 00:09:33,231
In the post-Vietnam era of the late 80s, the morale of U.S.

102
00:09:33,232 --> 00:09:35,280
forces was at an all-time low.

103
00:09:35,281 --> 00:09:41,420
The new M1 battle tank became a key symbol of the Reagan defense buildup

104
00:09:41,421 --> 00:09:45,420
And an easy target for those opposed to an increase in defense spending

105
00:09:45,421 --> 00:09:52,020
In this era of Cold War games and electronic warfare

106
00:09:52,021 --> 00:09:57,500
The new generation of battle tank required a completely different approach to crew training

107
00:09:57,501 --> 00:10:02,820
A radical new national training center was established

108
00:10:02,821 --> 00:10:05,160
OPFOR, opposing forces.

109
00:10:06,840 --> 00:10:09,780
There, real-life war games were carried out

110
00:10:09,781 --> 00:10:13,020
with American soldiers playing the role of Soviet forces.

111
00:10:14,300 --> 00:10:16,580
William Heidner took part in these exercises.

112
00:10:16,880 --> 00:10:19,260
You could buy a set of Soviet tanks

113
00:10:19,261 --> 00:10:23,100
to include a complete training package on how to use those tanks,

114
00:10:23,180 --> 00:10:26,440
and of course when their trainer showed up to teach Country X

115
00:10:26,441 --> 00:10:29,620
how to use these new tanks and infantry fighting vehicles,

116
00:10:30,140 --> 00:10:32,160
they were taught the Soviet methodology.

117
00:10:32,161 --> 00:10:37,100
If we were to go to war somewhere besides the central plains of Europe against the Warsaw Pact,

118
00:10:37,620 --> 00:10:41,440
you're still likely to run into the same types of equipment and the same types of tactics.

119
00:10:42,480 --> 00:10:48,360
We were known as Krasnovians from the fictional town or fictional country of Krasnovia.

120
00:10:48,860 --> 00:10:50,980
We had a solid O.D. green uniform.

121
00:10:51,880 --> 00:11:00,360
We would wear a Russian-style rank insignia and a Russian-style epaulet and branch insignia on our collars.

122
00:11:00,361 --> 00:11:05,160
The aim was to replicate combat as close as possible without spilling blood.

123
00:11:06,640 --> 00:11:12,960
To operate effectively as a team, modern tank crews require both a highly technical skill base

124
00:11:12,961 --> 00:11:17,240
and the mental agility to change roles if necessary when under fire.

125
00:11:18,400 --> 00:11:23,620
The training had to be improved because you have a faster tank.

126
00:11:24,400 --> 00:11:27,420
It traverses faster. It shoots faster.

127
00:11:27,421 --> 00:11:32,900
you have a knee switch you have your elevation uncoupled the training had to be more intense

128
00:11:32,901 --> 00:11:39,000
the gunner skills necessary taking a proper rip sight sight picture all these things had to be

129
00:11:39,001 --> 00:11:44,160
done in order to master that tank because it was fast we were uh we were like a family your tank

130
00:11:44,161 --> 00:11:49,560
crew is your family you're going through the same thing you're experiencing everything the next guy

131
00:11:49,561 --> 00:11:54,940
is you know it's not like hey he's got a mercedes and i got a pinto or something you know it's just

132
00:11:54,941 --> 00:12:03,640
you're all quite equal regardless of the rank the Abrams has a crew of four the

133
00:12:03,641 --> 00:12:07,620
commander sits above and behind the gunner in the main turret compartment

134
00:12:07,621 --> 00:12:16,440
with the loader to their left the driver sits alone in the front of the tank

135
00:12:16,441 --> 00:12:20,940
driver now I had to focus on driving with buttoned up and getting used to the

136
00:12:20,941 --> 00:12:24,460
speed because a lot of drivers during nighttime engagements or during the

137
00:12:24,461 --> 00:12:30,760
night would run so fast down a course road that they would lose track of where they were.

138
00:12:31,280 --> 00:12:37,220
You had to practice with him the proper platform in order for him to maintain a stable platform,

139
00:12:37,340 --> 00:12:41,770
to allow the crew to shoot well, and then also keep us on a course road.

140
00:12:41,771 --> 00:12:43,000
The loader had to be

141
00:12:43,001 --> 00:12:44,301
skilled in his position.

142
00:12:44,302 --> 00:12:47,880
He had to be able to pop that door open after the round was fired,

143
00:12:47,881 --> 00:12:49,888
pull around, load it, get it armed.

144
00:12:49,889 --> 00:12:53,100
We were looking for three seconds, three to four. That's

145
00:12:53,101 --> 00:12:53,819
what we wanted.

146
00:12:53,820 --> 00:12:57,700
After you fired a few rounds and you've got the caps rolling around on the floor,

147
00:12:58,400 --> 00:13:05,440
added that machine gun brass that's lying around, and I could feel the heat coming up through both

148
00:13:05,441 --> 00:13:11,580
the rubber chemical boots and into my regular boots, which was nice actually in the wintertime

149
00:13:11,581 --> 00:13:13,175
because it was cold.

150
00:13:13,176 --> 00:13:18,280
The commander has six periscopes that provide 360 degree vision.

151
00:13:18,281 --> 00:13:26,440
The thing with the periscopes is certainly you can navigate and fight down,

152
00:13:27,220 --> 00:13:34,840
but you still don't get all of the panorama, if you will, of being out and looking left and right to your tanks.

153
00:13:35,060 --> 00:13:37,640
In addition, they have what they call the commander's independent viewer,

154
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:41,860
where you have your own thermal device that you can swing left or right,

155
00:13:42,100 --> 00:13:45,320
and it's made it easier to be down inside the tank as opposed to outside.

156
00:13:45,321 --> 00:13:49,400
But still, old guys like me prefer being up out where we can look around.

157
00:13:54,580 --> 00:13:58,760
In the M1 Abrams, the gunner uses a thermal imaging system

158
00:13:58,761 --> 00:14:02,800
along with a laser rangefinder for extraordinarily accurate fire.

159
00:14:07,320 --> 00:14:11,340
But the designers decided not to incorporate an auto-loading system.

160
00:14:11,341 --> 00:14:25,253
If you put an autoloader in there with some hundred plus parts, how can that be as reliable as a single individual who's manhandling and loading the ammunition

161
00:14:25,254 --> 00:14:26,040
manually?

162
00:14:31,740 --> 00:14:35,000
The new tank had many other pioneering features.

163
00:14:35,001 --> 00:14:42,720
the greatest fear of any tanker since its invention was of being burnt alive or dying

164
00:14:42,721 --> 00:14:49,720
from exploding ammunition in a molten coffin some of the scariest images that were told about

165
00:14:49,721 --> 00:14:54,560
previous tanks were well you get you get hit with a heat round it's going to burn a hole through

166
00:14:54,561 --> 00:14:58,740
there it's going to be like a torch it's going to send molten armor all over the place and burn you

167
00:14:58,741 --> 00:15:02,740
up or you're going to get a kinetic energy round come flying through your tank and go bouncing

168
00:15:02,741 --> 00:15:05,800
around like a ping-pong ball, you know, and take half of you out the other side.

169
00:15:09,220 --> 00:15:14,620
To protect the tank crew, one of the Abrams' most innovative features was the blowout panels.

170
00:15:16,540 --> 00:15:21,640
As far as some of the protection that the M1 provided in taking a hit was the fact that

171
00:15:21,641 --> 00:15:26,820
you had blow-off panels that had been installed in the turret and shut heavy, thick metal

172
00:15:26,821 --> 00:15:32,520
doors so that if hit from the rear or the side of the turret where the ammunition would

173
00:15:32,521 --> 00:15:37,880
be ignited, those doors would withstand that so that in the event of a fire, the ammo would

174
00:15:37,881 --> 00:15:39,640
blow up and not into the crew compartment.

175
00:15:45,820 --> 00:15:50,660
For night vision, the driver uses an image intensifier starlight periscope.

176
00:15:50,661 --> 00:16:01,240
This enables a crew to engage targets rapidly and successfully, despite the darkness of night or daytime battlefield conditions of dust and smoke.

177
00:16:04,960 --> 00:16:08,400
Early models of the Abrams weighed in at 60 tons.

178
00:16:09,040 --> 00:16:13,680
They were powered by a gas turbine engine and could travel up to 45 miles per hour.

179
00:16:13,681 --> 00:16:19,160
The engine was much quieter and easier to maintain

180
00:16:19,161 --> 00:16:23,160
And with no smoke signature, the tank was much harder to detect

181
00:16:23,161 --> 00:16:30,260
Early models had a 105mm cannon

182
00:16:30,261 --> 00:16:37,680
But in the mid-80s, this was replaced by a much more powerful, German-made 120mm main gun

183
00:16:37,681 --> 00:16:45,360
The Abrams incorporated everything that latest advanced technology could offer

184
00:16:45,361 --> 00:16:49,500
But it still had its critics

185
00:16:49,501 --> 00:16:52,320
It was accused of being a gas guzzler

186
00:16:52,321 --> 00:16:56,680
Of being too heavy to transport to a new theater of war

187
00:16:56,681 --> 00:17:02,060
And at 4.3 million dollars apiece of being far too expensive

188
00:17:02,061 --> 00:17:06,100
During the Cold War

189
00:17:06,101 --> 00:17:10,500
the Abrams was intended as one of the key defensive weapons to protect Europe

190
00:17:10,501 --> 00:17:13,280
from a Soviet-led attack by the Communist bloc.

191
00:17:15,140 --> 00:17:19,620
In September 1982, during the NATO war games in Germany,

192
00:17:20,220 --> 00:17:22,060
the Abrams made its tactical debut.

193
00:17:23,620 --> 00:17:28,160
The impressive accuracy of the Abrams demonstrated its outstanding ability

194
00:17:28,161 --> 00:17:32,080
in a world of electronic warfare as a hunter and a killer.

195
00:17:32,760 --> 00:17:35,460
It was a turning point in armored warfare.

196
00:17:36,100 --> 00:17:42,940
We knew we had a winner across NATO probably three or four years into service, I would say by the mid-'80s.

197
00:17:43,380 --> 00:17:48,720
The tank could be doing 25 or 30 miles an hour over terrain, and you are literally shooting on the move.

198
00:17:48,721 --> 00:17:56,920
And when it could demonstrate that capability, that was when I think military professionals certainly realized that this is a fine-armored vehicle,

199
00:17:57,080 --> 00:17:58,880
and in fact, lives up to its bargain.

200
00:17:58,940 --> 00:18:03,520
What we didn't know was exactly how it matched up against its adversaries.

201
00:18:03,521 --> 00:18:12,660
By the late 1980s, the Cold War was coming to an end, and the tank was still untested in a real field of battle.

202
00:18:13,400 --> 00:18:15,420
All of that was about to change.

203
00:18:16,560 --> 00:18:22,528
The Abrams' baptism of fire would be in a very different conflict, a war that would reshape U.S.

204
00:18:22,529 --> 00:18:23,460
foreign policy.

205
00:18:24,140 --> 00:18:28,120
The echoes of which are with us today, the Gulf War.

206
00:18:28,121 --> 00:18:33,704
The M1 Abrams is so heavy that the largest cargo aircraft in the U.S.

207
00:18:33,705 --> 00:18:38,640
Air Force, the C-5 Galaxy, can only handle one tank per trip.

208
00:18:39,320 --> 00:18:42,500
M1 Abrams Supertank will return on Modern Marvels.

209
00:18:45,260 --> 00:18:49,880
For over ten years, the Abrams remained untested in the field of battle.

210
00:18:50,660 --> 00:18:57,000
Critics blasted its high production costs and questioned the combat survivability of its electronic warfare systems.

211
00:18:57,001 --> 00:19:00,880
But the Abrams was about to enter its trial by fire.

212
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:09,560
Beginning in 1980, Iraq and Iran had fought each other in a long and bloody war.

213
00:19:10,200 --> 00:19:14,120
Hundreds of thousands of people lost their lives or were horribly mutilated.

214
00:19:19,180 --> 00:19:24,260
In 1988, this brutal struggle came to an end with Iraq's victory.

215
00:19:24,261 --> 00:19:33,360
Saddam Hussein, the Iraqi dictator, built up a formidable war machine supplied by both the West and by the Soviets.

216
00:19:35,160 --> 00:19:37,300
Iraq was left with huge debts.

217
00:19:38,040 --> 00:19:42,120
Hussein accused neighboring oil-rich Kuwait of lowering oil prices.

218
00:19:43,080 --> 00:19:49,100
On August 2, 1990, the Iraqi dictator gave the order for Iraqi forces to invade Kuwait.

219
00:19:49,101 --> 00:19:54,820
Saddam Hussein was confident that the West would not respond

220
00:19:54,821 --> 00:19:57,040
but he had badly miscalculated

221
00:19:57,041 --> 00:20:00,540
President Bush Sr. acted quickly

222
00:20:00,541 --> 00:20:04,160
and put together a coalition of Western and Arab states

223
00:20:04,161 --> 00:20:07,360
committed to the overthrow of Saddam's rule in Kuwait

224
00:20:07,361 --> 00:20:13,660
a vast army began to deploy in the deserts of Arabia

225
00:20:13,661 --> 00:20:19,340
The Iraqi army had a considerable array of tanks

226
00:20:19,341 --> 00:20:21,960
Mostly purchased from the former Soviet Union

227
00:20:21,961 --> 00:20:26,140
Among these were a thousand modern Russian T-72s

228
00:20:26,141 --> 00:20:30,840
We certainly didn't underestimate them

229
00:20:30,841 --> 00:20:33,020
We thought there was going to be a very formidable enemy

230
00:20:33,021 --> 00:20:35,460
The feeling was that this could be a terrible fight

231
00:20:35,461 --> 00:20:37,320
With tremendous casualties

232
00:20:37,321 --> 00:20:39,500
And it could go for a very long time

233
00:20:39,501 --> 00:20:49,260
Half a million Americans and other allied troops began to arrive in Saudi Arabia in an enormous military buildup along the Iraqi border.

234
00:20:49,860 --> 00:20:53,880
The operation Desert Shield was led by General Norman Schwarzkopf.

235
00:20:55,320 --> 00:21:02,680
After a decade of development and training, the M1 Abrams was about to be put to the test in real battle combat.

236
00:21:02,681 --> 00:21:10,840
The latest model, the M1A1, was urgently deployed to the Gulf.

237
00:21:15,240 --> 00:21:20,120
Evolutionary in design, the new version had upgraded computerized gunnery.

238
00:21:20,740 --> 00:21:26,660
The stabilized gun mount enabled it to fire reliably while traveling at high speed over rough ground.

239
00:21:26,661 --> 00:21:38,060
The combination of maneuverability, longer range and night vision would give the crews the opportunity of defeating any tank in the Iraqi army.

240
00:21:41,860 --> 00:21:52,740
The Abrams tank crews needed to acclimate to the desert and for five months engaged in simulated training in preparation to take on the Iraqis' Russian T-72s.

241
00:21:52,741 --> 00:22:04,020
Dan Miller, captain of Iron Troop, had spent five years on war games, training to fight on the central plains of Germany.

242
00:22:06,700 --> 00:22:09,380
We had trained to fight the Warsaw Pact.

243
00:22:09,980 --> 00:22:14,320
So when we were discovering that we were going to Saudi Arabia, it was surprising.

244
00:22:14,820 --> 00:22:20,620
It wasn't so much that we were concerned, hey, we trained to fight these guys, now we're going to go fight the Iraqis.

245
00:22:20,621 --> 00:22:26,820
but hey at least we're going to get get in there and get in the fight because that's soldiers

246
00:22:26,821 --> 00:22:32,260
typically don't want to ride to the sound of the guns that's typical reactions went out into the

247
00:22:32,261 --> 00:22:40,500
desert and spent the next five months camped on our tanks in the desert waiting we were out there

248
00:22:40,501 --> 00:22:46,980
you know through the holidays and through Christmas and New Year's and right up into the

249
00:22:46,981 --> 00:22:55,840
We spent a lot of time maneuvering in the desert, practicing, especially with me coming out of basic training.

250
00:22:55,980 --> 00:23:00,380
I couldn't tell a tank cannon from a 50-calorie machine gun.

251
00:23:00,381 --> 00:23:03,260
I mean, I didn't know any of this stuff.

252
00:23:05,120 --> 00:23:08,880
But spending those five months out in the desert really gave us a chance to hone our skills.

253
00:23:09,660 --> 00:23:12,760
The tactics of we're going to change the long-range gunnery, we knew that.

254
00:23:12,920 --> 00:23:14,220
We're shooting across a flat plane.

255
00:23:16,980 --> 00:23:24,240
And we were moving in different formations than we ever moved before on a scale of vehicles that had not been done before.

256
00:23:25,200 --> 00:23:30,460
On January 16th, 1991, the air war against Iraq began.

257
00:23:31,420 --> 00:23:37,160
Over succeeding weeks, the high-precision aerial onslaught pummeled Iraq's military infrastructure.

258
00:23:37,640 --> 00:23:44,440
But Iraq's huge army, estimated that about one million men, remained undefeated on the ground.

259
00:23:46,980 --> 00:23:56,440
Some commanders argued that Iraq could be defeated by the air campaign alone.

260
00:23:56,800 --> 00:23:58,281
But President Bush Sr.

261
00:23:58,282 --> 00:24:04,880
decided on a short, sharp and decisive ground action determined to keep casualties to the minimum.

262
00:24:06,260 --> 00:24:09,600
The commanders now maneuvered men and machines into position.

263
00:24:10,880 --> 00:24:16,200
The new and unproven Abrams main battle tank was placed right on the front line.

264
00:24:16,980 --> 00:24:21,500
For the men who would crew these tanks, the battle ahead stirred very different emotions.

265
00:24:22,120 --> 00:24:27,400
My personal fear was that I would make a bad decision that would get my guys hurt or killed.

266
00:24:27,560 --> 00:24:34,580
I was more worried about that than I was about, you know, personal fear of getting hurt or whatever.

267
00:24:34,900 --> 00:24:36,440
You just don't think it's going to happen to me.

268
00:24:37,720 --> 00:24:45,360
We were prepared for anything, and I really wasn't too concerned with anything at all when I was on my tank.

269
00:24:45,361 --> 00:24:47,980
But just being away from it was scary to me.

270
00:24:48,420 --> 00:24:52,080
My biggest fear going over there was that I was going to be called on to kill people.

271
00:24:53,160 --> 00:24:59,620
I'd find myself in some village in Iraq somewhere gunning down children armed with RPGs or something like that.

272
00:25:00,280 --> 00:25:04,340
I don't think 18-year-olds, 19-year-olds think about the dangers of themselves.

273
00:25:04,860 --> 00:25:05,560
I can't get hurt.

274
00:25:07,840 --> 00:25:10,000
But the tank's critics remained vocal.

275
00:25:10,260 --> 00:25:14,720
Many believed that the Abrams would simply jam up in the sands of the desert.

276
00:25:15,360 --> 00:25:20,840
Under Saddam's threat of engaging the coalition in the mother of all battles,

277
00:25:21,180 --> 00:25:24,660
the young tank crews led their M1s onto the battlefield.

278
00:25:27,460 --> 00:25:33,160
The latest version of the Abrams tank incorporates steel-encased, depleted uranium armor

279
00:25:33,161 --> 00:25:37,200
and is protected against nuclear, biological, and chemical warfare.

280
00:25:37,920 --> 00:25:41,160
M1 Abrams Supertank will return on Modern Marvels.

281
00:25:41,161 --> 00:25:51,060
For five months, the coalition forces had practiced, patrolled, and maneuvered in preparation for battle.

282
00:25:52,040 --> 00:25:55,020
Desert Shield would soon no longer be an air war.

283
00:25:55,760 --> 00:25:59,640
For the Abrams crews, the days of simulated combat were over.

284
00:26:03,140 --> 00:26:08,340
The Iraqi army had been worn down by constant attacks from the coalition airstrikes.

285
00:26:08,341 --> 00:26:15,720
Conscripts stuck out in foxholes in the desert front line had little will left to fight

286
00:26:15,721 --> 00:26:21,460
Only the elite Republican Guard was ready and eager to take on the West

287
00:26:21,461 --> 00:26:25,420
With a force of 100,000 men and 1,000 tanks

288
00:26:29,020 --> 00:26:34,580
At 4 a.m. on February 24, 1991, the ground war was launched

289
00:26:34,581 --> 00:26:37,220
Operation Desert Storm

290
00:26:37,221 --> 00:26:42,480
Three, two, one, fire, fire, fire.

291
00:26:45,300 --> 00:26:51,600
Two marine divisions first attacked from the south, quickly penetrating what was known as the Saddam Line.

292
00:26:52,240 --> 00:26:58,300
They made rapid progress towards Kuwait City, slowed only by the sheer numbers of prisoners on the battlefield.

293
00:27:00,420 --> 00:27:06,000
Bad weather and smoke from the oil fields the Iraqis had set on fire obscured the desert.

294
00:27:07,220 --> 00:27:12,400
And everywhere, bombs and mines exploded, adding to the chaos.

295
00:27:19,020 --> 00:27:25,880
Dan Miller's cavalry troop was given a deadly mission, to seek, find, and engage the enemy.

296
00:27:27,040 --> 00:27:31,100
American cavalry is armed heavily enough to fight for information.

297
00:27:31,101 --> 00:27:38,100
We don't have to employ a standoff, look, develop information and send the report back.

298
00:27:38,101 --> 00:27:43,100
I can make you shoot at me, that tells me where you are, and I can either kill you myself,

299
00:27:43,101 --> 00:27:52,100
or pop back, report higher, and allow a higher level commander to pass a whole division through to come and kill you.

300
00:27:52,101 --> 00:27:56,100
That's the role of the American Cavalry. That's what we did that day.

301
00:28:01,100 --> 00:28:05,860
On February 26th, the first major tank engagement took place.

302
00:28:07,200 --> 00:28:12,840
The Battle of 73 Easting began with well dug in units of the Republican Guard.

303
00:28:15,540 --> 00:28:23,740
The Abrams tanks of Eagle Troop 2 Armored Cavalry led the assault by using their thermal visioning to identify the Iraqi tanks.

304
00:28:23,741 --> 00:28:34,540
for the abrams its first shooting war had phenomenal results

305
00:28:34,541 --> 00:28:42,820
it is a busy busy battlefield and you just you don't realize to me it seemed like it was four

306
00:28:42,821 --> 00:28:47,480
or five hours but it was really probably about an hour and a half engagement from start to finish

307
00:28:47,481 --> 00:28:53,540
with about 25 minutes of real, serious, intense, two-way range.

308
00:28:54,020 --> 00:28:56,360
They're shooting at us. We're shooting at them.

309
00:28:56,440 --> 00:28:57,940
They're bringing mortar rounds in on us.

310
00:28:58,560 --> 00:29:01,000
Typically, the Soviets, when you would put them under artillery,

311
00:29:01,900 --> 00:29:04,880
their doctrine was to close with you as fast as possible.

312
00:29:05,220 --> 00:29:06,800
That's exactly what I did with Iron Troopers.

313
00:29:06,940 --> 00:29:11,720
I said, okay, let's advance faster so that we could get in amongst them.

314
00:29:11,721 --> 00:29:15,020
So eventually, between our counter-battery fire,

315
00:29:15,021 --> 00:29:22,700
The fact that we were getting too close for them to use it, it took another tool out of his box that he could use against my guys.

316
00:29:24,300 --> 00:29:34,080
In just 23 minutes of combat, nine lone American tanks had taken on and wiped out all 38 Iraqi armored vehicles.

317
00:29:36,240 --> 00:29:42,000
They had cut a three-mile swath of destruction and taken out Iraq's most capable armor.

318
00:29:42,001 --> 00:29:49,120
I knew that the M1 was doing well from the first round we fired at a real target.

319
00:29:50,780 --> 00:29:55,820
I shot my first round and my gunner shot his first round at 2,900 meters, popped a turret off a tank.

320
00:29:56,120 --> 00:29:59,360
It went up an explosion and I'm going, yeah, this is neat stuff.

321
00:30:00,360 --> 00:30:02,500
That round really will blow a turret off.

322
00:30:03,000 --> 00:30:09,120
In the darkness, the Iraqi gunners fired wildly at the muzzle flashes from American tanks on the move.

323
00:30:12,000 --> 00:30:17,480
But burning Iraqi vehicles made the battlefield so bright that Steed's thermals whited out,

324
00:30:17,820 --> 00:30:21,740
a temporary blindness which made the tank vulnerable to enemy fire.

325
00:30:23,700 --> 00:30:25,640
We could see the damage that had been wreaked.

326
00:30:25,860 --> 00:30:28,020
You had burned out hoax, you had turrets flipped over.

327
00:30:29,000 --> 00:30:32,080
You had shell-shocked Iraqis.

328
00:30:32,960 --> 00:30:37,360
Steed was then ordered to stop and round up some Iraqi prisoners ahead of him.

329
00:30:38,320 --> 00:30:41,000
The rest of the battalion had driven off. They were gone.

330
00:30:41,001 --> 00:30:44,560
and my petunel leader was somewhere behind me, which I had no idea where he was.

331
00:30:45,380 --> 00:30:48,540
He stopped and signaled the Iraqis trying to surrender.

332
00:30:49,340 --> 00:30:52,680
Just then, a burning Iraqi Hulk silhouetted his tank.

333
00:30:56,320 --> 00:31:00,200
Hidden in the burning battlefield, just a thousand yards away,

334
00:31:00,201 --> 00:31:05,900
a lone T-72 crew sighted the illuminated Abrams, an unbelievable target.

335
00:31:05,901 --> 00:31:13,940
the t-72 had a perfect sight on the most vulnerable part of the abrams the side of the gun turret

336
00:31:13,941 --> 00:31:23,960
the enemy's auto loader swung into action with a deadly 125 millimeter heat round

337
00:31:23,961 --> 00:31:31,040
it was almost surreal it was kind of dreamlike in a way i'm sitting there looking in my front

338
00:31:31,041 --> 00:31:35,280
watching keeping an eye on the prisoners keeping an eye on vehicles of my right and left

339
00:31:35,281 --> 00:31:46,940
The round came in the left side of the tank.

340
00:31:47,420 --> 00:31:49,480
My loader got hit in the legs.

341
00:31:50,640 --> 00:31:51,780
He crawled over the side.

342
00:31:55,000 --> 00:31:59,380
By the time Steed regained consciousness, the fire extinguishers had worked,

343
00:31:59,640 --> 00:32:02,900
but the tank was still an easy target and in danger of exploding.

344
00:32:05,280 --> 00:32:07,520
Steve's first concern was for his gunner

345
00:32:09,820 --> 00:32:13,340
My gunner was trying to come up front of me because the black smoke was pouring out of the vehicle

346
00:32:13,341 --> 00:32:16,880
And I was trying to get him under control so that we could get up on top and see what's wrong

347
00:32:18,360 --> 00:32:20,218
I reached down in there to get him.

348
00:32:20,219 --> 00:32:24,200
I turned around and noticed my blast doors were open. I was concerned about

349
00:32:24,960 --> 00:32:26,960
ammunition being cooked

350
00:32:28,400 --> 00:32:30,400
I tried to push it shut I

351
00:32:30,401 --> 00:32:35,760
I was able to close the door, get it re-secured, got him out.

352
00:32:36,380 --> 00:32:40,480
I had a long spaghetti cord, so I could still talk on the radio,

353
00:32:40,620 --> 00:32:43,940
so I was talking to my commander, explaining to him that we'd been hit

354
00:32:43,941 --> 00:32:46,840
and giving him a status on my people.

355
00:32:49,400 --> 00:32:51,460
And then I went back to the tank trying to get it started

356
00:32:51,461 --> 00:32:52,760
because we needed to ride out of there.

357
00:32:53,360 --> 00:32:55,180
All the warning lights, everything was lit up.

358
00:32:55,181 --> 00:32:56,820
It would turn over, but it wouldn't drink.

359
00:32:57,420 --> 00:33:01,660
So at that point, we started trying to figure out how we're going to get out of here.

360
00:33:03,560 --> 00:33:06,620
Steed and his injured crew were in serious danger.

361
00:33:07,500 --> 00:33:11,060
After hiding from the enemy at the rear of the tank for nearly an hour,

362
00:33:11,580 --> 00:33:15,520
one of Steed's platoons sighted the stricken Abrams and came to their aid.

363
00:33:16,060 --> 00:33:16,780
I was kind of pissed.

364
00:33:17,980 --> 00:33:21,820
You know, I was pissed I was in a position that caused this to begin with.

365
00:33:22,640 --> 00:33:26,560
And it was fair to say that I wanted to continue to do more.

366
00:33:26,820 --> 00:33:29,780
to keep that from happening to somebody else.

367
00:33:31,600 --> 00:33:33,460
With his wounded crew safe,

368
00:33:33,980 --> 00:33:36,260
Steed insisted on taking command of the tank,

369
00:33:36,440 --> 00:33:37,840
and he returned to the battle.

370
00:33:40,580 --> 00:33:42,280
For his heroic action,

371
00:33:42,680 --> 00:33:46,120
Tony Steed was later awarded the Bronze Star for valor.

372
00:33:47,600 --> 00:33:49,960
It's been obvious since tank warfare began to me,

373
00:33:50,060 --> 00:33:53,400
but you don't stop a rolling tank in the middle of a battle area.

374
00:33:53,540 --> 00:33:56,080
We're not POW wagons.

375
00:33:56,820 --> 00:33:59,180
So that changed the SOP real quick.

376
00:33:59,440 --> 00:34:02,760
Now, within the platoon, within the unit, within the battalion,

377
00:34:02,860 --> 00:34:06,960
the orders were issued so that that wouldn't happen again.

378
00:34:08,060 --> 00:34:10,200
At dawn the following morning,

379
00:34:10,680 --> 00:34:14,200
the Iraqi army appeared to be withdrawing to the north towards Basra.

380
00:34:15,000 --> 00:34:19,960
But the tanks of the Republican Guard had dug in behind a six-mile ridge of high ground.

381
00:34:26,820 --> 00:34:31,600
the Abrams would face its toughest opposition yet.

382
00:34:34,200 --> 00:34:37,600
In the largest armored conflict since World War II,

383
00:34:38,120 --> 00:34:40,760
the Abrams was about to play a major role

384
00:34:40,761 --> 00:34:43,900
in one of the great tank battles of the 20th century,

385
00:34:44,560 --> 00:34:46,440
the Battle of Medina Ridge.

386
00:34:49,400 --> 00:34:52,460
Three versions of the Abrams tank are currently in service.

387
00:34:52,461 --> 00:34:55,940
The original M1 model, dating from the early 1980s,

388
00:34:55,941 --> 00:35:00,380
and two newer versions designated M1A1 and M1A2.

389
00:35:01,040 --> 00:35:04,400
M1 Abrams Supertank will return on Modern Marvels.

390
00:35:07,060 --> 00:35:11,840
The largest single tank engagement of the World Wind War of Desert Storm

391
00:35:11,841 --> 00:35:17,660
took place on the fourth day of fighting at Medina Ridge on February 27, 1991.

392
00:35:21,180 --> 00:35:25,760
The M1 Abrams tanks of the U.S. 1st and 3rd Armored Divisions

393
00:35:25,761 --> 00:35:32,460
now faced off against 300 T-72 tanks of the elite battle-hardened Republican Guard.

394
00:35:33,380 --> 00:35:41,400
Despite smoke and sand, the thermal sites could identify the Iraqi T-72 tanks long before the enemy could see them.

395
00:35:41,960 --> 00:35:47,460
Even where they had been dug in behind several feet of sand, the Iraqi tanks could be picked out.

396
00:35:47,461 --> 00:35:56,700
Incredibly, the Abrams was able to hit the T-72s at 3,500 yards

397
00:35:56,701 --> 00:36:01,860
well before the Abrams was visible to the optical sights of the Soviet-made tanks.

398
00:36:02,640 --> 00:36:09,320
When the Abrams used a depleted uranium round, it could destroy a T-72 with a single shell.

399
00:36:09,960 --> 00:36:12,560
The Iraqi tanks exploded in a sheet of flame.

400
00:36:17,460 --> 00:36:30,380
The Iraqi tank crews tried firing back at the Abrams tanks when they could see them, but they were out of range.

401
00:36:31,080 --> 00:36:37,740
After five months of waiting in the desert, Loder Sheehan Miles was prepping his tank when he was ordered into action.

402
00:36:38,320 --> 00:36:45,100
We were supposed to cross the border at midnight, and as I was prepping the tank and getting everything ready around 11 o'clock in the morning,

403
00:36:45,101 --> 00:36:51,420
and all of a sudden my platoon leader comes running back across the assembly area yelling,

404
00:36:51,600 --> 00:36:53,060
everybody get ready, we're leaving now.

405
00:36:53,900 --> 00:36:58,680
And on five minutes notice we loaded everything up in the tank as quick as we could

406
00:36:58,681 --> 00:37:00,180
and got in and moved out.

407
00:37:00,640 --> 00:37:05,580
So when it came time to fire in combat, of course the ammo door was clogged with sand

408
00:37:05,581 --> 00:37:06,440
and I couldn't open it.

409
00:37:06,780 --> 00:37:11,180
And the breach was filled with sand and the main gun wouldn't fire.

410
00:37:11,560 --> 00:37:14,600
And meanwhile, people are shooting at us.

411
00:37:14,601 --> 00:37:16,540
and nothing was working.

412
00:37:16,920 --> 00:37:18,080
After I hit the circuit breaker,

413
00:37:18,820 --> 00:37:23,300
I'm halfway back into my position when the gunner fired

414
00:37:23,301 --> 00:37:26,880
and it went off that time and practically knocked me out of my seat.

415
00:37:28,160 --> 00:37:29,760
Under cover of darkness,

416
00:37:30,280 --> 00:37:34,580
the Abrams' superior gunnery range and night weapon systems were decisive.

417
00:37:35,980 --> 00:37:39,180
At Medina Ridge, when we first came into contact,

418
00:37:40,000 --> 00:37:42,660
the Red Platoon had actually identified the targets

419
00:37:42,661 --> 00:37:49,600
and we were then given the okay to engage and we were told at that time that there was nothing in

420
00:37:49,601 --> 00:37:56,214
front of us but enemies. So we pulled up online and started engaging targets.

421
00:37:56,215 --> 00:37:57,760
Our weapon systems

422
00:37:57,761 --> 00:38:05,280
can reach out and touch at a distance and our sights are much better than the Soviet-made tanks

423
00:38:05,281 --> 00:38:11,680
and I wasn't scared at all or threatened by any other vehicle on the battlefield.

424
00:38:12,660 --> 00:38:15,720
I was just so intrigued, you know.

425
00:38:16,240 --> 00:38:17,140
I was looking around.

426
00:38:17,280 --> 00:38:19,460
This is the first time I've ever been away from home.

427
00:38:19,960 --> 00:38:21,440
I was actually in combat.

428
00:38:21,880 --> 00:38:23,020
I felt so distant.

429
00:38:24,240 --> 00:38:26,220
During the Battle of Medina Ridge,

430
00:38:26,540 --> 00:38:29,740
the Abrams gun had proved superior to that of the T-72.

431
00:38:31,080 --> 00:38:32,720
It was like a turkey shoot,

432
00:38:33,080 --> 00:38:34,760
and with their tanks being shot up,

433
00:38:35,020 --> 00:38:37,520
the Iraqi resolve to fight began to fade.

434
00:38:40,400 --> 00:38:41,920
By the end of the battle,

435
00:38:41,921 --> 00:38:44,800
only four Abrams tanks had taken direct hits.

436
00:38:45,420 --> 00:38:48,780
But due to the durability of its armor, all crews survived.

437
00:38:49,820 --> 00:38:54,920
For the majority of the Abrams crews, tank warfare was long-range and impersonal.

438
00:38:56,300 --> 00:38:59,880
Loader Gene Benson, however, had a rare face-to-face encounter

439
00:38:59,881 --> 00:39:02,440
with an Iraqi soldier of the Republican Guard.

440
00:39:03,300 --> 00:39:09,080
He was wounded, so as we're coming up to him, I got my .240 on him.

441
00:39:09,081 --> 00:39:14,400
you know for if he had a weapon or anything like that i would be ready and i was already locked and

442
00:39:14,401 --> 00:39:21,820
loaded and ready to go but he had his hand up and he was waving like help me i almost grabbed my

443
00:39:21,821 --> 00:39:27,160
camera and took a picture of it you know as opposed to shooting him but then i said hey no

444
00:39:27,161 --> 00:39:34,340
you know we were told this is degrading and i said okay well we'll just pass by him close

445
00:39:34,341 --> 00:39:40,380
These encounters with the horror of war had a profound effect on the 18-year-old loader Sheehan Miles.

446
00:39:40,960 --> 00:39:49,020
We did have one incident where some trucks came rushing through our position in the middle of the night.

447
00:39:49,660 --> 00:39:54,460
And, you know, we did what you do when unknown trucks come rushing towards your position in the middle of the night.

448
00:39:54,700 --> 00:39:55,560
We shot them.

449
00:39:56,080 --> 00:40:00,140
And one of them was a fuel truck, and it caught the other truck on fire, and that was full of people.

450
00:40:00,141 --> 00:40:03,680
and they all came out running and they were on fire

451
00:40:03,681 --> 00:40:07,580
and we machine gunned them and killed them all.

452
00:40:08,200 --> 00:40:11,440
You know, again, it was tough to live with for a long time.

453
00:40:12,420 --> 00:40:16,860
I was angry because the way we prosecuted that particular mission,

454
00:40:17,520 --> 00:40:20,040
you know, sitting in a tank company down this crowded road

455
00:40:20,041 --> 00:40:23,380
that was already covered in burning and destroyed equipment

456
00:40:23,381 --> 00:40:26,280
that helicopters had fired up, just didn't make any sense to me.

457
00:40:26,281 --> 00:40:31,180
but I wasn't an officer and I'm not the kind of person who makes those calls.

458
00:40:33,340 --> 00:40:37,300
The destructive power of the battalions of tanks was overwhelming.

459
00:40:38,140 --> 00:40:44,520
In just over two hours, 186 Iraqi tanks and 127 armored vehicles,

460
00:40:44,840 --> 00:40:47,220
the cream of Saddam's forces, were destroyed.

461
00:40:48,160 --> 00:40:49,860
The battle was a turning point.

462
00:40:49,861 --> 00:40:53,640
The Iraqi army was in full-scale retreat towards Basra.

463
00:40:56,280 --> 00:41:03,280
President Bush Sr. declared a cease-fire at midnight on February 28.

464
00:41:03,281 --> 00:41:11,280
After 100 hours of the ground war, General Schwarzkopf and the coalition forces had won a stunning victory.

465
00:41:11,281 --> 00:41:15,280
Kuwait was now free.

466
00:41:15,281 --> 00:41:21,280
After the war, it was estimated that the Iraqis had lost 3,700 tanks.

467
00:41:21,281 --> 00:41:25,740
Over 20,000 men were killed and tens of thousands wounded.

468
00:41:27,480 --> 00:41:35,620
Miraculously, during the whole conflict, only 18 Abrams had been disabled and just four knocked out by Iraqi anti-tank fire.

469
00:41:36,560 --> 00:41:41,060
Not a single member of an Abrams tank crew had been killed by enemy fire.

470
00:41:42,240 --> 00:41:50,340
The combination of the supertank's advanced technology and the superior training and discipline of the crews had won the day.

471
00:41:51,280 --> 00:41:54,900
12 years later.

472
00:41:55,380 --> 00:42:00,540
Saddam Hussein and his sons must leave Iraq within 48 hours.

473
00:42:01,160 --> 00:42:02,900
March 20, 2003.

474
00:42:03,600 --> 00:42:05,740
America is again at war with Iraq.

475
00:42:06,660 --> 00:42:11,300
And once more, the M1 Abrams will prove itself a formidable weapon.

476
00:42:13,380 --> 00:42:16,940
Within 24 hours of the start of Operation Iraqi Freedom,

477
00:42:16,941 --> 00:42:21,860
The 3rd Infantry's 7th Cavalry is rolling, unopposed across the desert.

478
00:42:23,360 --> 00:42:25,240
Their destination, Baghdad.

479
00:42:26,480 --> 00:42:32,340
In the first three days, they move with breathtaking speed, covering some 300 miles.

480
00:42:32,940 --> 00:42:35,800
Even their brutal sandstorms barely slow their progress.

481
00:42:38,400 --> 00:42:43,140
Iraqi pickup trucks, mounted with primitive missiles and automatic rifles, charge the tanks.

482
00:42:43,940 --> 00:42:46,240
Their attacks are mostly suicidal.

483
00:42:46,940 --> 00:42:55,940
There will be no spectacular tank-on-tank battles in this war.

484
00:42:55,941 --> 00:42:59,260
But not all Abrams will escape unscathed.

485
00:42:59,261 --> 00:43:04,100
And one Abrams driver is shot and killed, and the tank he is operating falls into a

486
00:43:04,101 --> 00:43:05,100
river.

487
00:43:05,101 --> 00:43:08,940
His entire crew perishes with him.

488
00:43:08,941 --> 00:43:14,880
After days of intense aerial bombardment, the M1 Abrams rolls into Saddam International

489
00:43:14,881 --> 00:43:22,520
Airport, enters the grounds of a presidential palace, and then moves into the heart of the

490
00:43:22,521 --> 00:43:23,221
city.

491
00:43:25,260 --> 00:43:27,580
This is psychological warfare.

492
00:43:28,060 --> 00:43:30,000
The Army calls it Thunder Run.

493
00:43:32,180 --> 00:43:40,320
After only three weeks, the war is declared over, and the legacy of the M1 Abrams is secure.

494
00:43:40,321 --> 00:43:46,000
We all know that the M1's strengths are its armor, its maneuverability.

495
00:43:46,800 --> 00:43:49,460
You can fire offensively or defensively.

496
00:43:49,860 --> 00:43:52,300
It was just awesome.

497
00:43:55,400 --> 00:44:00,300
After all that happened, I would say that the Abrams' survivability ain't number one.

498
00:44:00,960 --> 00:44:02,260
It kept my crew from getting killed.

